# 19. Preliminary National Findings

### Finding 1 — Conservation capacity is distributed.

No single institution possesses the workforce necessary to manage the country's entire conservation system.

### Finding 2 — Headcount is an inadequate capacity metric.

The relevant unit is mission capability, not employee count.

### Finding 3 — Recent federal workforce reductions provide evidence of capacity risk.

Recent GAO reviews document substantial workforce reductions at major natural-resource agencies and instances where agencies had not yet completed mission-based assessments of required staffing and skills.

### Finding 4 — Specialized skills may matter more than aggregate staffing.

A shortage of hydrologists, fisheries scientists, contracting specialists, fire personnel, GIS analysts, or enforcement officers can constrain an entire program even when overall staffing appears adequate.

### Finding 5 — Administrative capacity is conservation capacity.

Procurement, grants, contracting, finance, HR, and IT can determine whether ecological programs are actually implemented.

### Finding 6 — Institutional memory has conservation value.

Turnover can remove knowledge that is difficult to recreate through hiring or contracting.

### Finding 7 — Partnerships expand capacity but do not eliminate the need for internal expertise.

Government agencies still need sufficient staff to define objectives, manage partnerships, oversee contracts, interpret results, and maintain accountability.

### Finding 8 — State and Tribal capacity remains a major national research gap.

Federal workforce data are comparatively developed. Comparable national information for state, Tribal, local, and regional conservation capacity is much less standardized.

### Finding 9 — Workforce capacity should be connected to ecological workload.

A staffing number has little meaning without knowing the responsibilities assigned to that workforce.

### Finding 10 — Strategic workforce planning should become a conservation performance function.

The central question should be whether institutions have the right people, skills, organizational structure, and continuity to fulfill their responsibilities.

