# 22. Preliminary Policy Hypotheses

The baseline suggests several hypotheses for later testing.

### H1 — Mission-based workforce planning is superior to across-the-board staffing targets.

Staff requirements should be derived from responsibilities and workload.

### H2 — Critical-skill shortages matter more than aggregate vacancies.

### H3 — Administrative capacity should be treated as part of conservation capacity.

### H4 — Internal expertise should be retained for mission-critical functions even where contracting is appropriate.

### H5 — Institutional memory should be treated as a conservation asset.

### H6 — Conservation agencies increasingly require hybrid ecological and computational expertise.

### H7 — Workforce allocation should reflect ecological and administrative workload rather than population alone.

### H8 — Partnerships increase effective capacity only when organizations possess sufficient coordination capacity.

### H9 — Adaptive management requires organizational flexibility as well as scientific information.

### H10 — Workforce investments should ultimately be evaluated against implementation and ecological outcomes.

